UPDATE tarot_cards 
SET meaning_upright = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: The Six of Wands represents the absolute archetype of "Triumphant Integration"—the phase where the kinetic friction of the Five has been successfully processed, refined, and converted into a clear, high-velocity vector of external validation. Moving beyond the struggle of creative conflict, the Six signifies the state of "Public Sovereignty," where the individual’s internal mastery is finally recognized, mirrored, and amplified by the collective field. Visually depicted as a victorious figure riding through a crowd on horseback, wearing a laurel wreath and carrying a staff adorned with the same, this archetype embodies the concept of "Resonant Achievement"—the realization that your hard-won integrity has produced a result that is not only functional but socially and structurally significant. Within the complex architecture of your consciousness, this is the stage of "alignment completion," where the feedback loops between your inner intent, your disciplined execution, and the external manifest world are synchronized at a peak level of performance. When the Six of Wands appears, your system is registering a "Verified Success" status; the work of the previous phases has culminated in a moment of undeniable progress. This is the archetype of the "Heroic Return," where the wisdom gained in the heat of conflict is now being utilized to lead, inspire, and define the standard for your specific domain of operation. It commands you to accept this recognition with the humility of one who knows the cost of the victory, and the confidence of one who understands that this success is not an end, but a validated platform for the next, even more ambitious, expansion. You are being tasked with the essential work of "leadership alignment": ensuring that as you step into the light of external success, you do not lose the internal connection to the source of your drive, treating your triumph not as an ego-reward, but as a proof-of-concept for your soul’s continued evolution. To inhabit the Six of Wands is to master the art of "integrated magnetism," understanding that by successfully navigating the struggle and coming out the other side with your integrity intact, you have created a beacon that naturally draws toward you the resources, connections, and opportunities necessary for the next, higher-order cycle of your manifestation.',
meaning_reversed = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: When the Six of Wands appears in the reversed position, it acts as a high-intensity signal of "performative hubris," "imposter syndrome," or the systemic collapse of your external validation loop—a state where the appearance of success is masking a profound, internal sense of failure or a detachment from the actual, foundational work that made the success possible in the first place. The reversal suggests that you have become obsessed with the "optics of victory," running a high-priority, high-cost maintenance script that prioritizes the maintenance of your public persona—your status, your "wins," your social capital—at the direct expense of your genuine, internal alignment. You are essentially "riding a horse that isn’t moving," exerting massive amounts of energy to sustain a narrative of achievement while internally, you are feeling fragmented, hollow, or terrified that the facade will inevitably shatter under the weight of its own lack of substance. This creates a massive, systemic misalignment: your outer world may be designed to look like a Six of Wands—acclaim, recognition, outward stability—but the frequency is failing, leading to a profound, chronic sense of alienation, intellectual dishonesty, and the gnawing awareness that you are no longer the one driving your own life, but a captive of the very image you created. From an analytical perspective, this is a clear sign that you are leaking your life force into a "feedback loop of vanity"; you are prioritizing external applause over the internal, high-vibration truth that is the only actual source of your power. The reversed Six of Wands exposes the hidden, egoic fear that drives this behavior: the paralyzing terror of being "found out," and the corresponding fear that if you were to stop the performance, you would be left with nothing but the uncomfortable, unvarnished reality of your own, work-in-progress state. This reversal commands an immediate, radical intervention: you must stop the output of these performative scripts, perform a brutal audit of your current "victories," and confront the reality of why you are so hesitant to prioritize the integrity of the process over the reward of the result. It is a demand to stop the self-deception that keeps you tethered to a failing narrative, and to initiate the move toward a genuine, humble, and often terrifying reclamation of your own, authentic truth. You are being tasked with the uncomfortable, yet essential, work of stripping away the wreath—accepting that the "stuckness" you feel is the direct result of having prioritized the recognition of the world over the resonance of your own soul, and realizing that until you are willing to let the false glory collapse, you will continue to be starved by the very applause you have worked so hard to procure.'
WHERE id = 20;
